cia officer mark polymeropoulos went to  moscow in 2017 for meetings with russian  intelligence officials  paula meropoulos never thought he'd come  back to the u.s with a career-ending  traumatic brain injury it's been four  years of a 24 7 headache which is you  know not only is it painful physically  but just from a mental health  perspective it's pretty it's pretty  debilitating so while i've gotten better  because the treatment i've received at  walter reed's national intrepid center  of excellence that's one of the world's  leading traumatic brain injury programs  i'm still not there yet so i'm still  suffering palomaropola says he woke up  in his hotel room dizzy ears ringing and  suffering a never-ending migraine  a year earlier diplomats at the u.s  embassy in havana reported similar  symptoms  at first the u.s government considered  the cases anomalies but five years later  at least 200 u.s government officials  and some of their relatives are  struggling to recover from unexplained  health incidents also known as havana  syndrome  the biden administration says these  cases have spurred it to find the cause  when you sit down with with our people  and hear  [Applause]  what's happened to them  how they've been affected  it's very very powerful  and it only reinforces  in me  the absolute conviction that we need to  do everything possible  for our people to care for them  to protect them  to get to the bottom of what happened  the assumption the officials are being  attacked with weapons that use lasers or  microwaves instead of bullets similar to  what's being demonstrated here  the injuries have turned up in vienna in  bogota in guangzhou and in hanoi  just before vice president kamala  harris's visit in august  some speculate russia is behind the  attacks a charge the kremlin denies  still cia director bill burns recently  warned moscow there would be payback if  true  the trump administration also blamed  cuba expelling some of their diplomats  in 2017.  mark palmeropoulos and his lawyer  believe the government should tell the  public all it knows we don't know  what the intelligence community knows  truly about these  incidents  and if revealing certain information  would cause our country greater problems  but i'd like to think  that hopefully we are the victim  and solely the victim in this situation  and that we have something that we can  do in response  doctors say the cases resemble the  traumatic brain injuries u.s forces  suffered in ied attacks during the iraq  war  congress recently passed the havana act  to make sure the injured get the care  they need but it doesn't address the  growing unease some feel about serving  abroad  mark palamaropoulos says the cia has  stood by him in the past four years but  he wants the u.s to send a message these  attacks won't be tolerated they say this  is an act of war and so we're going to  have to respond appropriately so you  know i don't think this is just simple  sanctions um certainly you don't want to  start you know world war iii over this  but there's going to be have to have to  be some really tough measures taken  against our adversary you know if it's  russia this is this is serious business  now  a call to action to protect those who  thought they knew the risks that came  with their jobs  rosalind jordan al jazeera washington
 

